-- title:
--   Good operators generate at a multiplicity-one eigenvector
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero, $V$ a finite-dimensional $L$-vector space which is also a $\mathbb{Q}$-module compatibly (a scalar tower $\mathbb{Q}$, $L$, $V$), and let $V_{\mathbb{Q}} \subseteq V$ be a $\mathbb{Q}$-submodule forming a rational form in the sense that (i) any finite family $v : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to V$ of vectors lying in $V_{\mathbb{Q}}$ which is linearly independent over $\mathbb{Q}$ is linearly independent over $L$, and (ii) $V_{\mathbb{Q}}$ spans $V$ over $L$. Let $\rho : \mathbb{Z}[X_i : i \in \sigma] \to \mathrm{End}_L(V)$ be a ring homomorphism from a multivariate polynomial ring over $\mathbb{Z}$ (so the operators $\rho(X_i)$ commute) such that each $\rho(X_i)$ preserves $V_{\mathbb{Q}}$. Let $G \subseteq \sigma$ be a set of indices such that for each $i \in G$ there is a separable $P \in \mathbb{Q}[T]$ with $P$, base-changed to $L$, annihilating $\rho(X_i)$. Let $\lambda : \sigma \to L$ and let $v_0 \neq 0$ satisfy $\rho(X_i)v_0 = \lambda_i v_0$ for all $i \in G$, with multiplicity one: every $w \in V$ with $\rho(X_i)w = \lambda_i w$ for all $i \in G$ is an $L$-multiple of $v_0$. Then for every $t \in \mathbb{Z}[X_i : i \in \sigma]$ there exist $s, u$ in the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra generated by $\{X_i : i \in G\}$ with $s(\lambda) \neq 0$ and $\rho(s)\rho(t) = \rho(u)$.
--
--   This is the linear algebra behind the classical assertion that, on the line spanned by a newform satisfying multiplicity one, the anemic Hecke algebra generated by the good operators captures every operator of the family after inverting a good operator non-vanishing at the eigenvalue system. It is used in the construction of the Galois representation attached to a primitive form, via [`CuspForm.IsPrimitiveForm.exists_mem_adjoin_good_aeval_ne_zero_mul_smul_eq_smul_jOne`](thm.html#CuspForm.IsPrimitiveForm.exists_mem_adjoin_good_aeval_ne_zero_mul_smul_eq_smul_jOne).
